config:
schema_version: 1
identity:
  model: gru
  version: v012
  parent: gru_v011
  status: smoke
description: 'Single-variable follow-up to v011: same soft mask, same 3-epoch schedule,
  but the periodic recurrent-state reset fires every 2,000 rows instead of every 4,000.'
hypothesis: 'The reset period is a knob, not a threshold, and 2,000 rows beats 4,000.
  dozken''s public reset2k scores 0.6569 against their reset4k, so shortening our
  horizon should gain rather than cost. Mechanism: across 20k rows the recurrent state
  degrades (their Q1 0.70 -> Q4 0.638) and a zero state beats the model''s own 10k-row
  state, so more frequent resets keep the model nearer its best regime. If 2,000 instead
  loses to 4,000 then the horizon is near-optimal at 4,000 and this lever is spent.'
change_vs_parent: 'Shorter absolute reset horizon: 4,000 rows -> 2,000 rows. Implemented
  as chunk_len 2000 -> 1000 with state_reset 4000 -> 2000. Unchanged: the soft mask
  (loss.mask_model), aux weighted-MSE 0.05, EMA 0.999, architecture 2x128 (192,258
  params), seed 1337, 3-epoch cosine schedule.

  WHY chunk_len MUST MOVE TOO, and the resulting confound: the trainer enforces state_reset
  = k * chunk_len with k >= 2, because a reset at every chunk boundary would discard
  all carried state and silently leave the model with no memory beyond one chunk.
  At chunk_len 2000 the shortest legal period is therefore 4000, which is exactly
  v011''s value, so a shorter horizon is unreachable without also halving chunk_len.
  Keeping k=2 preserves the 2-chunks-of-carry ratio the trainer enforces and changes
  the absolute horizon from 4,000 to 2,000, which is also dozken''s literal reset
  period. The confound is that chunk_len sets truncated-BPTT granularity, so this
  run is not a pure period change; a (chunk_len 2000, state_reset 6000) run would
  isolate the ratio and test the opposite direction. 1000 divides 20,000 exactly,
  so no chunk crosses a sequence boundary.'
